# audioserver reading MediaTek's vendor.* audio properties.
#
# 983 denials in one boot, and this one is on the critical path to a booting
# device rather than being log noise. Measured 2026-08-11 (build 34):
#
#   avc: denied { read } scontext=u:r:audioserver:s0
#        tcontext=u:object_r:vendor_default_prop:s0 tclass=file
#
# What it costs: audioserver never finishes publishing AudioPolicyService --
# its log loops "AudioPolicyService not published, waiting..." while
# AudioFlinger gets as far as openOutput() -- so system_server blocks in
#
#   AudioProductStrategy.native_list_audio_product_strategies (native)
#   AudioDeviceBroker.initRoutingStrategyIds(AudioDeviceBroker.java:194)
#
# until Watchdog kills it, forever. The boot animation never ends.
#
# Note the shape of the misdirection here, because it cost time: `init.svc.
# audioserver` reads `restarting`, which invites the conclusion that audioserver
# is crashing on its own. It is not -- zygote restarts audioserver in its
# `onrestart` list, so every system_server death restarts it. The restart is a
# symptom of the very loop it is causing.
#
# vendor_default_prop is the catch-all label for vendor properties with no more
# specific type, and MediaTek's audio HAL configuration lives there
# (persist.vendor.audiohal.*, vendor.audiohal.*). Read only: audioserver has no
# business setting them.
#
# 🔴 SILENCED, NOT GRANTED -- and the reason matters more than the rule.
#
# Two attempts to allow this were both refused by AOSP's own neverallows:
#
#   allow ... :file r_file_perms   ->  neverallow domain property_type
#                                      (file (ioctl lock))
#                                      private/property.te:100
#   get_prop(audioserver, ...)     ->  neverallow base_typeattr_528
#                                      vendor_default_prop (file (... open ...))
#                                      public/property.te:306
#
# The second one is the real answer. audioserver is a CORE domain, and core
# domains are forbidden from reading vendor_default_prop at all -- that is a
# deliberate Treble boundary, not a gap in this tree's policy. Stock's
# audioserver gets exactly the same denial and stock boots.
#
# So the 983 records per boot are noise by design, and my reading of them as the
# cause of AudioPolicyService failing to publish was wrong. A denial can be both
# real and irrelevant; volume is not evidence of importance. If audioserver
# genuinely needed one of these properties, the fix would be to give that
# property its own vendor type that core domains may read -- never to punch
# through the boundary for the catch-all type.
dontaudit audioserver vendor_default_prop:file r_file_perms;
